<sn><md>#### What Is a Dose?

&gt; In toxicology, a dose refers to the `quantity of a substance` that an `organism is exposed` to. 

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/dose_diff_in_organism.png)

But simply saying "10 milligrams of chemical X" doesn't tell us much on its own. A 10 mg exposure will affect a mouse very differently than a human adult. That's why dose is almost always `expressed relative to body weight` – most commonly as `milligrams of substance per kilogram of body weight` (++mg/kg++).

</md></sn><sn><md>#### Important Assumptions in the Dose-Response Relationship

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/dose-response-graph1.png)

1. There is always a ++dose below++ which `no response occurs` or can be measured.
2. Once a ++maximum response++ is reached, any `further increases` in the dose will not result in any increased effects.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Dose-Response Connection
A basic idea in toxicology and pharmacology, the dose-response connection denotes the `quantitative link between an organism's biological response and the dose of a drug` that is supplied or encountered.Knowing the dose-response connection is crucial for 
- evaluating `risks`, 
- establishing `exposure limits`, and 
- directing regulatory choices, regardless of whether one is examining the toxicity of environmental contaminants or the therapeutic efficacy of pharmaceuticals.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Expressing Dose 

**LD50**

The LD50, for instance, is expressed as the `weight of chemical` administered `per kilogram body weight` of the test animal, along with the species and route of exposure used. So an LD50 (oral, rat) of **5 mg/kg** means that 5 milligrams per kilogram of body weight `caused death in half the tested rat population`.

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/ld50_diagram.png)
</md></sn><sn><md>### Types of Dose

It's important to distinguish between different types of dose:

- **Administered dose** – the `total amount` of a substance given to an organism.
- **Absorbed dose** – the `portion` that `actually enters` the bloodstream and reaches target tissues.
- **Total dose** – the quantity of a substance administered to an individual `over a period of time` or in several individual doses, which is particularly important when `evaluating cumulative poisons`.

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/types_of_dose_diagram.png)</md></sn>